WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.215
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.215 --> 00:00:03.300
AMAH EDOH: How is
Africa made to mean?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.640
How has it been made to
mean, and what is associated

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.020
with this idea of Africa?

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.300
I mean, it's weird to talk
about it as this one place,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.508
but that's exactly the point.

00:00:10.508 --> 00:00:12.540
How is it that we've
come to talk about Africa

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.840
as a thing, a place in the
world, when in fact, it

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.860
includes over 50 countries,
and thousands of languages,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.750
and millions of people, right?

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.250
SARAH HANSEN: Today on the
podcast, a conversation

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.350
about teaching a categorically
misunderstood concept, Africa.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.120
Our guest today
found inspiration

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.210
for bold new ways of teaching
through her own experiences

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.470
as a student.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.840
She recognized
throughout her education

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.900
the ability for teachers
to empower or inhibit

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.643
their students.

00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:42.810
AMAH EDOH: What's made the
biggest difference for me

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.330
in the classroom
as a student has

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.860
been instructors
who made me feel

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.020
like I had something to offer.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.150
And the lack of that does
a lot of damage, right?

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.350
When you're having
instructors who

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.940
make you feel like you
have nothing to offer,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:54.850
that's actually damaging.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.767
It's not just neutral.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.310
It's damaging.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.850
So it's really
important for me to sort

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.150
of valorize students'
voices and to show them

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.445
that they're doing it.

00:01:05.445 --> 00:01:07.320
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.020
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.520
I'm your host Sarah Hansen
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.410
In this episode,
I take you with me

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:20.190
to meet Amah Edoh, an Assistant
Professor of African Studies

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.360
at MIT.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.160
Professor Edoh's
research centers

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.700
around the complex array of
cultures, traditions, nations,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.480
and ideas known as Africa,
and how Africa as a concept

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.640
is produced through
material practices,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.660
things like music, ritual,
performance, and dress.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.190
We'll learn how she
engages students

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.650
in unpacking the
concept of Africa

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.320
through the very creative
practices that make it.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.500
So what does it look
like to challenge

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.100
the meaning we've prescribed
to a place as rich and diverse

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.720
as Africa?

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.090
We'll pick up our conversation
with Professor Edoh's

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.290
explanation.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.690
AMAH EDOH: You know, we're
used to speaking of Africa

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.250
as a place--

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.200
and not just any
place, but a place

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.520
that's burdened with a
lot of ideas and values

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.060
that historically have been
negative, to put it simply.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.240
And so what my research
and my teaching centers on

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.770
is about interrogating that.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.020
So first, making visible
the ways that Africa

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.330
is made to mean, so that's
what I mean with Africa

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.890
as a category of thought.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.740
To understand that Africa
means more than just

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.018
this geographical space.

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.560
It's also all these
countries and all

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.420
this very complicated history.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.750
But then to see the
ways that this valence

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.970
that "Africa,"
quote, unquote, has,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.860
the way that that plays out in
all aspects of life, really.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.772
So broadly speaking,
it's like the question

00:02:39.772 --> 00:02:41.730
of thinking about Africa
as category of thought

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.395
is understanding the politics
that are involved in engaging

00:02:46.395 --> 00:02:48.270
with the continent in
an intellectual manner,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.160
politically, and so on.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.790
In this course, we use creative
practices-- so the arts,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.170
popular culture--

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.490
as a way in for two reasons.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.513
One, because there's
a way that, you know,

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.430
I'm not crazy about the
term "popular culture"

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.740
because it's a way that
it seems to devalue it,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.920
and it's just kind of fun.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.003
Yet, it animates so much
of our daily experiences,

00:03:10.003 --> 00:03:11.462
no matter who you
are and no matter

00:03:11.462 --> 00:03:12.810
where you are in the world.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.070
So on one hand, the purpose
or goal of using this as a way

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.680
into asking these more kind of
theoretical and philosophical

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.620
questions about Africa
as a category of thought

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.760
is to recognize that these
everyday practices are actually

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.880
extremely rich and
just as political

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.520
as any other form of engagement.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.830
But then more
specifically to Africa

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.620
is the fact that
right now, Africa

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.100
is sort of having this moment
where its signification is

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.180
appearing to shift.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.670
Part of it is understanding
whether it's actually shifting.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.310
But where all the stories
we had about the continent

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.500
used to be about war,
and disease, and famine,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.740
and dysfunction,
simply speaking,

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.150
now, there is also another
discourse where Africa's hot.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.560
Africa is sort of the future.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.990
Africa is where all of these new
possibilities are playing out.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.490
So whether it's within
the business realm

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:12.660
or it's within the
arts, it's this idea

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.430
that Africa is
the last frontier.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.149
So there is a way that
Africa's got a new prominence

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:20.160
on the global stage,
and that a lot of that

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.710
is playing out in
the realm of what

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.250
I call creative practice, so
the arts and popular culture.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.040
And that's why I thought that
it would be a particularly

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.190
good angle to take in
addressing these sort of loftier

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.650
questions, and seeing the
ways that they play out

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.210
in the day-to-day.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.250
And also through beautiful
and pleasant objects,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.130
and pleasant things to engage
with, things that are moving,

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.650
and that are inspiring,
which I think

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:48.630
opens us up to thinking and
to processing differently when

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:52.665
we're moved in this other way.

00:04:52.665 --> 00:04:55.620
SARAH HANSEN: It
makes me wonder how

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:01.810
this specific case of looking at
Africa as a category of thought

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:03.670
and looking at
creative practices,

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:07.270
did it open for students
new ways of seeing

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.760
the world in general?

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.120
Does this kind of teaching
offer new pathways

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.725
for critical thinking that's
applicable in many fields?

00:05:17.725 --> 00:05:18.730
AMAH EDOH: I'd hope so.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.640
I mean, my god, like, that's
our dream as educators, right?

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.240
That whatever we do in the
classroom in this very limited

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.460
setting or this limited scale
manifests for our students

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.337
so that they're able to take
it into their experiences way

00:05:32.337 --> 00:05:34.420
beyond the classroom and
beyond the specific topic

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.717
of the course.

00:05:35.717 --> 00:05:37.300
When I think about
the students that I

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.490
had in the class last year,
it was about 12 students,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:45.355
and about half of them
were African, so Africans

00:05:45.355 --> 00:05:46.230
of different variety.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.272
So Africans from the
continent, Africans who were

00:05:48.272 --> 00:05:49.930
first-generation Americans.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.260
And then the other
half were American

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.620
of varying ethnicities.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.690
And I would say that
for all of them--

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.630
well, certainly for
my African students,

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.520
it was a way that they,
I think it's safe to say

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.760
were there because they
were interested in engaging

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.580
with this part of
their experience

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:12.010
in a different realm,
to actually study it

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.880
in the classroom instead of it
just being kind of what they're

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.390
immersed in their
day-to-day lives

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.110
when they're back
home, and so on.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.000
And for the non-African
students, some of them

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.550
were going to be working in
Africa during the summer,

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.220
or had just come back from
being on the continent,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.220
and just wanted to kind
of learn more generally

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.530
about the continent.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.690
So to what extent did
being in the class

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:37.233
broaden their perspectives
or kind of give them

00:06:37.233 --> 00:06:39.400
tools that they could use
beyond this specific topic

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:40.840
after the class?

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.690
I think the class might have
helped them get language

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:47.560
to articulate some
of these questions

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.830
about power, essentially.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:53.380
And if not always
to find answers,

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.080
to at least be able
to notice patterns

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.460
and sort of describe
what they're seeing.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.450
So often, we have a feeling
that something's not right,

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.170
or that there's
something going on here,

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.650
but we don't quite have
the language for it.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:09.130
And I think that that's what
a class like this aims to do

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:12.300
is to give students the
language to articulate

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.050
these things that they
might be picking up

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.010
on in different ways.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.020
SARAH HANSEN: So what does it
look like to empower students

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:24.100
to follow a feeling, to develop
the language to negotiate

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.560
that feeling into theory?

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.650
AMAH EDOH: So we met once a
week and we focused each session

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.230
on a different form of
cultural production,

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:35.930
or a different theme.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:36.885
So we had performance.

00:07:36.885 --> 00:07:39.260
And then within performance,
we had performance as dress,

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.093
and then we had literature,
and we had film,

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:45.280
and we had science
and technology.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.170
And so for each unit,
new questions came up.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:49.900
But the thread that
cut across them

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.790
was the same, which
was a question of,

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:54.100
how is Africa being made
to mean in the world,

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:58.150
and how are African
cultural producers engaging

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.135
in that process of making Africa
to mean through their practice?

00:08:01.135 --> 00:08:02.510
And, of course,
and the questions

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:03.885
that come up that
are, like, what

00:08:03.885 --> 00:08:07.480
are the specific issues that
arise with this particular form

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.130
of cultural production, right?

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:11.830
So for theater, what does
it mean, for instance,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.130
to produce a play for
an audience in Nigeria

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.520
to can perform that play in
Cambridge, Massachusetts?

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:19.840
What are the politics of that?

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.530
What are the
questions that come up

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:26.320
in carrying this object from
one space into the next?

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.630
The goal was to both reflect
through each of these cases

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.419
this underlying question
that the course was after,

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:38.980
but then to show the nuances
or the specificities that

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:43.090
apply to each particular
form of creative production.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.130
And then ultimately
to say, what do we

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:49.000
gain by juxtaposing
these different things?

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.400
What can we learn about ideas
of technological innovation

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.640
in or for the continent
by looking at them

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.990
alongside the production
of ethnographic portraits

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:05.990
in the 19th century in
South Africa, for instance?

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.630
Is there a way that
these two cases

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:11.210
can elucidate one another?

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.370
And I think yes.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.560
I mean, there is a reason
that I did it that way.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.390
But yeah, and I think that
there was also another agenda

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:23.140
behind it is that being at MIT
in particular, where we tend

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:29.890
to fetishize technology
and consider it sometimes

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:33.340
in the realm outside
of other material

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:38.920
practices, other
ways of knowing,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:42.102
one of my personal missions is
to show that it's no different.

00:09:42.102 --> 00:09:43.060
They're just different.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:43.840
They're all objects.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:44.600
They're all ways of doing.

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:45.975
They're always of
knowing, right?

00:09:45.975 --> 00:09:48.070
So whether we're talking
about plant healing

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.450
or developing the
next nano whatever,

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.610
we can think about these
things next to each other,

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:56.140
no matter where
they're happening.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.330
And so I think the ways
that case studies are

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.610
useful is that they allow
you to both kind of dig

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.910
deep with that
particular situation,

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:09.380
bring questions to the fore,
and then next time, kind of

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:12.890
consider the ways that
those questions can apply,

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:14.390
or when new questions
arise when you

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:16.160
look at a different situation.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.590
SARAH HANSEN: You
mentioned performance.

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:21.590
And could you speak a
little bit about how

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:23.315
students attended
performances out

00:10:23.315 --> 00:10:24.815
in the world as
part of this course?

00:10:24.815 --> 00:10:26.520
AMAH EDOH: Yeah.

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.810
So that kind of has to
happen serendipitously.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.880
It just so happened
to be that at the time

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:37.122
that I was teaching this
class, this show came, was--

00:10:37.122 --> 00:10:39.080
I don't even remember
how I found out about it.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.420
I think from a mailing
list or a friend told me.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.230
I don't know, but this
play was coming to Harvard.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.780
And similarly, that
Alain Gomis was going

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.420
to be coming to Harvard also.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.010
And so in the case
of Alain Gomis,

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.490
I had just seen his film at a
conference a few months before,

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:58.610
one of his films, and
absolutely adored it,

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.890
and so I'd wanted to teach it.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:04.788
And it was an amazing
opportunity to have them here,

00:11:04.788 --> 00:11:06.830
because he would also be
speaking about the film.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:11.450
And so in both cases,
it was an opportunity.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.420
I saw these outings as an
opportunity for students

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:22.820
to engage with these materials
sort of in a more social way.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.320
So it's one thing
to be in your room

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.740
and to just watch a
film on your laptop.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:29.210
It's a different thing
to be in an auditorium

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:33.080
with various kinds of
people to kind of pick up

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.942
on the energy that's around
you, how other people around you

00:11:35.942 --> 00:11:38.150
are responding to different
parts of the film, right?

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.100
That's part of the
experience also.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.230
Not to mention that to
hear the filmmaker tell you

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:45.250
what they were thinking when
they created this thing,

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:49.640
and how other audiences
responded to it, and so on.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.260
And then, of course, just the
experience of leaving MIT,

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:55.440
I think, is really useful.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:59.030
Like, I remember when I was an
undergrad, Harvard seemed far.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:01.850
It's really not.

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.580
And so I think part of
our duty as educators also

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.640
is to help broaden our
students' horizons,

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:11.060
both in the classroom, but also
by encouraging them to just

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.670
go explore a little
bit beyond campus.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:15.530
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.690
Professor Edoh also
has a unique way

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.270
of broadening students'
horizons within the classroom

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.820
as well by reshaping
their relationship

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:26.570
with academic texts, all part
of an intentional process

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:31.145
to eradicate the barrier placed
between academic work and life.

00:12:31.145 --> 00:12:33.140
AMAH EDOH: So when
I-- you know, I

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:34.690
was a student for
a very long time.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:36.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:41.360
And I was very frequently
frustrated by the fact

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.757
that academic texts
often feel like they're

00:12:44.757 --> 00:12:45.965
written to not be understood.

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:46.720
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:48.428
AMAH EDOH: And this
made me really angry,

00:12:48.428 --> 00:12:50.390
because when I was in
grad school for my PhD,

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:53.000
I thought, OK, I've been
in school for many years.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.240
How is it that this still
makes no sense, right?

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.730
Like, if it makes no
sense to me, like,

00:12:57.730 --> 00:12:59.030
what are we doing here?

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.130
What is the point of
the academic enterprise

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.440
if we produce work that
can't be understood?

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.110
And so this has been a gripe
of mine for a very long time.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.780
And it was all the
more frustrating

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:11.300
when there was no space
to express that, right?

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.800
Because I think the danger when
you're a student oftentimes,

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.680
especially at
elite institutions,

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:16.970
is that you internalize.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.800
Like, when you don't
understand, you

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.240
think there's something
wrong with you, essentially.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:20.960
You're not good enough.

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:21.960
You're not smart enough.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.350
[BABBLING]

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:25.940
When it's a structural
issue, and it's actually

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.310
on the readers-- on the
writers to write better work,

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.680
write more clearly.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.800
So then that requires, I
think, an infrastructure

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.310
to support students
in processing

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:39.230
this often opaque work.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.480
And to me, that's how I was
thinking about the reading

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:42.010
responses.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:46.460
So as a way to just when you're
forced to sit down and write

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.770
something about what you
wrote, even if it's just like,

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:49.830
I don't understand it.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.080
These are all the questions
that are coming up for me.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.750
It already pushes
you along, like,

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.850
kind of your understanding
for yourself,

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:58.040
and then also gives me
as the instructor a way

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:02.210
to know where you're at
and what I need to bring

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:03.463
in the classroom discussion.

00:14:03.463 --> 00:14:04.880
So what I asked
students to do was

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:09.430
to try to summarize the
key points of the argument,

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:10.847
and then they were
free to take it

00:14:10.847 --> 00:14:12.138
in at any number of directions.

00:14:12.138 --> 00:14:13.910
They could either
relate it to other work

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:15.950
that we've read in class.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:17.870
They could relate it
to stuff in the news

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:20.090
that they're seeing around it,

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:22.138
seeing around them,
and kind of use

00:14:22.138 --> 00:14:23.930
what was coming up in
the readings as a way

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.347
to make sense of
these things they

00:14:25.347 --> 00:14:27.240
were observing around them.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:30.560
Or kind of relate it to their
personal experience, something

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.810
going on in their own lives.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.860
And what I wanted to
do there was, again,

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.780
to show that what we do in
the classroom is not separate.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:41.900
Academic work isn't
separate from life, right?

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.540
And it's not something that's
only accessible to some people,

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:45.040
right?

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.030
We are always theorizing.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:49.760
Making sense of our experience
is about theorizing.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.952
And so to offer
possibilities for students

00:14:51.952 --> 00:14:54.410
to make these links between
what they're reading for school

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:56.480
and what they're
experiencing in the world

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.700
around them, if
we can do that, I

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.260
think we're in great
shape as educators.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.350
I really love being able to
point to something that they've

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.240
said and make them
argue it, right?

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:09.040
Or in presenting my
lessons, if I can say, yeah,

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.010
as you brought up in
your paper, this thing

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.240
is going on in the reading.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.330
That's exactly right.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:17.730
And we also see-- so
to show that, one, I'm

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.020
taking their work seriously.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:19.853
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:15:19.853 --> 00:15:22.830
AMAH EDOH: And also, that
they have something to offer.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:25.950
To me, what's made the
biggest difference for me

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.250
in the classroom
as a student has

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:30.810
been instructors
who made me feel

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:34.850
like I had something to offer.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:37.523
And the lack of that does
a lot of damage, right?

00:15:37.523 --> 00:15:38.940
When you're having
instructors who

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.550
make you feel like you
have nothing to offer,

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:41.460
that's actually damaging.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:42.377
It's not just neutral.

00:15:42.377 --> 00:15:43.900
It's damaging.

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.470
So it's really
important for me to sort

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.693
of valorize students'
voices and to show them

00:15:49.693 --> 00:15:50.610
that they're doing it.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:51.792
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:51.792 --> 00:15:53.250
SARAH HANSEN: Part
of why we wanted

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.370
to profile Professor Edoh's
teaching of her Global Africa

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.830
Class was her
willingness to try things

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:01.140
in the classroom
that were new to her,

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.030
such as inviting
students to participate

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.160
in creative production.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.560
This approach culminated
in a final project

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:13.160
assignment that, to say the
least, exceeded expectations.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.930
In the final project
assignment, students

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.690
were asked to examine how
Africa's place in the world

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.120
is negotiated through
creative production.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.960
What kind of challenges did
students take on in this work?

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:26.890
AMAH EDOH: My poor students.

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.290
I say my poor students because
this was an experiment.

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:30.540
I thought it'd be really cool.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:34.640
I mean, it seemed like the
right thing in the course

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:38.790
where we're looking at people
who are making creative work,

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:40.570
doing creative work
in different arenas.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:42.930
It seemed like the
right way to culminate

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:44.775
to ask students to do
the same themselves.

00:16:44.775 --> 00:16:45.570
SARAH HANSEN: Yeah.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.945
AMAH EDOH: And yeah, I had no
idea how it would turn out,

00:16:47.945 --> 00:16:51.480
because I didn't know what they
were bringing to the table,

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.990
what particular skills they
had, like creative skills.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.370
But I want to believe--

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.560
this is something I try
to enact in my own life--

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.420
that we all have
creative skills.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.900
And so whatever you have,
we can do something with it.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.473
And bless them, because they
were game for the experiment,

00:17:06.473 --> 00:17:07.890
because I hadn't
done this before.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.650
I didn't know how
it was going to go.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.895
But I asked them to pick a topic
that they were interested in,

00:17:13.895 --> 00:17:16.020
either something we'd
already talked about in class

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:21.089
that they wanted to take father,
or another topic altogether.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:23.640
And the key was to use this
object, this thing that they

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:28.410
would make as a way to think
through these questions

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.700
about Africa's
place in the world.

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:32.872
It's quite broad.

00:17:32.872 --> 00:17:35.080
But I was so happy with the
things they came up with.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:36.070
It was wonderful.

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:38.940
It was so fun.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:41.700
We had anything from, like,
there was one student who

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:43.082
was interested in plants.

00:17:43.082 --> 00:17:44.790
And she picked this
plant, one of these--

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.590
I think it's like
the ficus plant.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:48.840
It's some plant that's really
sexy right now in, like,

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:52.140
the interior decorating world.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.900
And it's a plant that grows on
the side of the street in West

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:55.800
Africa, and so on.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.530
And so she did this cool
project or she took the plant

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:01.170
and looked at the
different ways that it

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:06.120
is made to mean as a kind
of luxury decoration item,

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.030
versus in central Africa,
where it's used for--

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:12.125
I forget-- like, some part of
the tree's use for bark cloth.

00:18:12.125 --> 00:18:13.500
And so from there,
you could talk

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.333
about the circulation
of objects and the way

00:18:15.333 --> 00:18:17.340
that value gets
articulated, and so on.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:22.080
Or other students who
wanted to talk about the way

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.060
that tradition--
that's kind of, yeah,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.790
related-- the way that
tradition gets articulated.

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:34.575
And they were interested in
looking at wedding ceremonies

00:18:34.575 --> 00:18:37.200
sort of in the Nigerian-American
context, where people are kind

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.630
of walking across these
different cultures, these two

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.240
different cultural realms.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.090
And to consider what
you keep of a tradition

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:46.380
and what you update.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.380
What does it mean to treat
women a particular way

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:51.440
and men in a particular way?

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:52.560
What should you uphold?

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:57.475
What should you
resist, given when

00:18:57.475 --> 00:18:59.100
you are a Nigerian-American,
and you're

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:01.558
kind of beholden to you belong
to these different cultures?

00:19:01.558 --> 00:19:03.930
And so what they did, which
I thought was so brilliant,

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:06.510
was that they put
together a picture book.

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:11.160
Because also, one of the classes
had talked about these photo

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:17.010
novels as a genre of image and
storytelling, image production

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.210
and storytelling.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.120
And so they used that
format that we talked about

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:24.030
in the class and took
the theme that they

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:29.100
were interested in around
the politics of tradition

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.300
in Igbo weddings.

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:32.498
And she wrote a story
that featured them

00:19:32.498 --> 00:19:33.790
grappling with these questions.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:36.030
So it was quite meta,
because they told you--

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.468
they talked about the topic
they were interested in,

00:19:38.468 --> 00:19:40.260
but then they also
showed you the questions

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:41.220
that it brought up.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.650
It wasn't the resolved thing.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:45.820
And it was meant
to be circulated,

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:48.360
and it was an object that could
be used to spur questions,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.250
and so on.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:51.660
And so it was fantastic.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:53.070
I so enjoyed them.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.490
We had a coffee ceremony in
class, like an Ethiopian coffee

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.820
ceremony, and the sort of
questions that brings up about,

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:04.260
what does it take to actually
enact what the ceremony is

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.783
supposed to enact when you
take it out of Ethiopia

00:20:06.783 --> 00:20:08.700
and we take it into a
dorm room, for instance,

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:10.950
and you only have certain
implements to use?

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:15.845
To what extent does it retain
its value, its significance?

00:20:15.845 --> 00:20:17.922
But it was wonderful
to see them start out

00:20:17.922 --> 00:20:20.380
being like, oh my god, I have
no idea what I'm going to do,

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.710
and then just figuring
it out, and coming up

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:23.930
with something amazing.

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:25.680
SARAH HANSEN: We
actually have the example

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:28.363
of the wedding conflict
book on the OCW website.

00:20:28.363 --> 00:20:30.030
We've linked to it
in the show notes, so

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:31.770
be sure to take a look.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:33.810
To close our
conversation, I wanted

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.510
to see what advice Amah
has for other educators

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:40.410
looking to employ some of her
techniques in their classes.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:43.230
What are her strategies for
keeping her class engaging,

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.930
thoughtful, and illuminating?

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:49.590
AMAH EDOH: So one key
thing is being clear

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:53.190
on what the core issue
is in the class, right?

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.350
So what's the core?

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:58.290
What's the question that
animates this class?

00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:02.490
And for me, it's useful to kind
of know that and have that,

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.120
because that helps me sort
of adjust as needed, right?

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:07.870
So to figure out,
OK, what do I need?

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:10.680
What do I what do I want these
students to come away with,

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:11.880
no matter what?

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.310
So that if what I have planned
is not taking us there,

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:18.210
then I can try something
else that might get us there.

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:19.710
So being clear on
this question-- so

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.960
for this class, global
Africa, creative cultures,

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.180
the idea was to examine
Africa's place in the world

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:26.130
as being made visible
through different kinds

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:27.450
of creative practice.

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:30.150
So I structured it
according to things

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.180
that I was familiar with from
my work or interested in,

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:34.740
and that I wanted
to explore more.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.410
So that's one way
to go about deciding

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.870
what the different units
are going to be, right?

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.480
So I'm interested in
dress, for instance,

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:44.450
and so performance as
dress was something

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:45.990
I was really into doing.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.650
So the pieces of the class
can be completely different.

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:52.345
They can focus on different
creative practices.

00:21:52.345 --> 00:21:54.720
You can spend more than one
week on a particular creative

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:55.520
practice, right?

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:57.390
So the building blocks
can be different.

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:01.050
And as long as the
core question is clear,

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:04.470
then you can sort of tailor
that to your interests,

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.240
but then also to
what's available.

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:11.160
What's cool about the
notion of creative practice

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:12.903
is that it's very broad.

00:22:12.903 --> 00:22:14.570
It can be as broad
as you want it to be.

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:19.185
So it can be restructured
any number of ways

00:22:19.185 --> 00:22:21.060
depending on the resources
that are available

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:24.150
and the questions that
you or your students

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:25.830
are interested in.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:28.260
And tied to that
is the fact that--

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:30.533
someone told me, a colleague
told me once that--

00:22:30.533 --> 00:22:31.200
we were talking.

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.630
We're both first-year
faculty, and we're both

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:36.220
talking about how exhausted
we were all the time.

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:40.290
And she said someone told her
that teaching is essentially

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.790
like stand-up or improv, right?

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:45.590
Like, you get up
there, and you're on.

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.158
And it's improv, because
you are responding

00:22:48.158 --> 00:22:49.700
to what your students
are giving you,

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.492
and you have no idea
what it's going to be.

00:22:51.492 --> 00:22:53.940
No matter how
well-planned it is,

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:55.830
you have no idea
where it might end up,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:56.920
and you have to respond.

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:58.360
So it's not just that
you're there taking it,

00:22:58.360 --> 00:22:59.820
but based on what
you're getting,

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.500
you have to figure
out where to take it

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:03.330
in order to come
back to conclusions

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:05.520
that you think you need
to end at, and also

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:09.390
be open to that not happening.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.075
And when you're new,
you just don't know.

00:23:12.075 --> 00:23:14.700
And when you've been a student,
and you've had these professors

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:17.158
up front who seemed to know
exactly what they're doing what

00:23:17.158 --> 00:23:19.920
they're talking
about, you don't fully

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:22.110
appreciate the fact
that it's a lot

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:25.350
of intuitive and
improvisational work.

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.990
And so you can be surprised
at how taxing it is, you know?

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:32.160
So at the end of a three-hour
class, I mean, I was wasted.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:36.360
You're just so tired because
it requires so much energy.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:40.110
So that's something to just kind
of be aware of, which also then

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.303
can inform your preparation.

00:23:43.303 --> 00:23:44.970
So to know, what you
need to do in order

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:48.390
to have enough to stock
up, to fill up your tank

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:51.990
so that you can then do this
three-hour improv performance.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:56.220
And generally, the
less tightly designed

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:59.220
it is, the better
things work out.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:00.750
This is what I
found and what I've

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:03.747
heard other colleagues say, that
the days when they were running

00:24:03.747 --> 00:24:06.330
from meeting to meeting and they
didn't quite have enough time

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:08.270
to finish their course prep
were actually the days where

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:10.063
the class went really
well, because they

00:24:10.063 --> 00:24:12.480
had no choice but to be there,
and listen to the students,

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:15.780
and meet them where
they were, and follow

00:24:15.780 --> 00:24:18.210
that organic progression
of the conversation

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:20.560
instead of sticking
to their script.

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:21.060
Yeah.

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:24.200
So I think you know the key
pieces of advice I would give

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:28.240
are kind of one, flexibility,
both in the approach

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:31.290
to teaching and recognizing
the flexibility that you

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:35.580
have in designing the course
and choosing what you put in it.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:39.150
And tied to this flexibility,
this experimental approach

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:43.060
to building it and to
adjusting it as it goes.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:47.460
And then as a broader kind of
teaching philosophy thing, what

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:50.310
I said about using the
time in the classroom

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:53.940
as a time to build our
students' confidence,

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.920
to instill in our students
a sense that they know,

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.960
that they are capable
of doing this work,

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.660
to validate, to
affirm, and to use that

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:06.180
as a starting point
for what it is that we

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:09.180
want to impart onto them.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:14.120
But really, to start to kind
of undo some of the damage

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:17.710
that academic work has
done for so many peoples,

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.740
which is that it's alienating.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:26.070
And so to try to make it
less alienating, to say,

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:29.623
we can do this, and we know how
to do this, and we're learning.

00:25:29.623 --> 00:25:32.040
And it's OK to not know, because
we're in school to learn,

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:32.350
right?

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:32.680
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:33.910
AMAH EDOH: We're not
supposed to know everything

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:35.370
when we come here.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:37.890
To make that as
part of the mission

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.620
beyond both, beyond the
content of the class itself,

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:44.670
to just say, it's
OK to not know,

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:46.560
and to not know this
particular material.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.260
But we know how to learn, and
that's what we're here for.

